---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';

const LOCALE = 'en' as const;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Who writes opameti.me, why the English and Serbian sides carry different things, and how to get in touch."
  lang={LOCALE}
  altHref="/o-sajtu/"
>
  <header class="post__header">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p class="post__lead">
      Open-source Home Assistant integrations and small tools, plus beginner
      guides written in Serbian.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="prose">
    <h2>Two languages, different content</h2>
    <p>
      This is not a translated site, and that is deliberate. The
      <a href="/">Serbian side</a> carries beginner guides — installing Home
      Assistant, choosing Zigbee hardware, writing a first automation — because
      those did not exist in Serbian. Translating them into English would put
      them against a market that already has a thousand of each, and they would
      help nobody.
    </p>
    <p>
      The English side carries the <strong>projects</strong> instead: integrations
      for devices and services nobody else supported, and the notes that explain
      how they work. For those there is no competition, because in most cases
      there is no alternative either.
    </p>

    <h2>What gets built here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Home Assistant integrations</strong> for Serbian utility and
        telecom providers, bintec-elmeg access points, Candy/Haier appliances and
        Bambu Lab printers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Music Assistant players</strong> for webOS and Tizen televisions.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Small macOS tools</strong>, such as bringing up Sidecar
        automatically on a Mac with no display.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      All of it is on <a href="https://github.com/zonya" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>,
      free, and MIT or similar. Everything runs locally — no vendor cloud, no
      account, no telemetry. It exists because it is used here every day, which
      is also why it keeps working.
    </p>

    <h2>How it is written</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Limitations are stated.</strong> Where something is built on a
        private API or can break on an OS update, that is in the text — not in
        an issue you find later.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>From practice.</strong> Recommendations are things actually run
        here, not things that read well on a product page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Corrections are welcome</strong> and outrank everything else. If
        a guide does not work for you, the guide is wrong until proven otherwise.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Money</h2>
    <p>
      The site costs about €5-6 a month to run. The aim is for it to cover
      itself through affiliate links to hardware and possibly ads, which means
      one commitment to you: <strong>a recommendation never changes because of a
      commission</strong>. Where an article contains an affiliate link, it says
      so. Details are in the <a href="/en/privacy/">privacy policy</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The software is free and stays free. If a project saved you an afternoon,
      <a href="https://ko-fi.com/zonya2026" rel="noopener"> a coffee</a> is a
      nice way to say so — and a bug report is worth more.
    </p>

    <h2>Who is behind it</h2>
    <p>
      The site is built and maintained by <strong>{site.company.name}</strong>, an
      IT company whose work includes smart-home installations, home servers and
      networks. The guides come out of that work — mostly answers to questions
      that keep coming up.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions, corrections and topic suggestions are all welcome. Write to{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a>, or open an issue on the
      relevant repository.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
